1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
SPHERExLens — finding what changed in NASA's SPHEREx all-
sky infrared survey.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:27,000
One image shows where things are. Two visits show what
changed. Subtract them and the sky cancels, leaving a
dipole if a source moved, or a monopole if it brightened
or faded — but instrumental residuals outnumber the real
events a thousand to one.

3
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The task: two 180-pixel cut-outs, one significance map, a
six-sigma threshold — 109 detections. Each one must be
identified as catalogue star, asteroid, artefact, or
candidate. Public data only, every number reproducible.

4
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Four questions: is the map calibrated? Can we separate
dipoles from monopoles at three arcseconds? How much do
catalogues explain? And what did we miss?

5
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Forty years of subtraction — kernel fitting, then discrete
kernels, then ZOGY: closed-form Fourier subtraction with
an analytic null. That null is why we can quote a false-
alarm rate.

6
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Every survey detects, vetoes, triages, then hands to a
human. Brokers do this for optical alerts — nothing end-
to-end was published for SPHEREx pixels.

7
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:33,000
SPHEREx: a twenty-centimetre telescope, 102 bands from
0.75 to 5 microns, six detector arrays, six FITS
extensions per cut-out, six-month cadence. Every pixel has
its own colour.

8
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:45,000
We fetch five-megabyte stamps rather than seventy-megabyte
frames: eighteen fields, 246 cut-outs, each accepted only
with margin and few flagged pixels.

9
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Seven subsystems — ingest, imaging, ZOGY, triage, machine
learning, deep fields, evaluation — sharing one JSON
contract and 21,429 lines of Python.

10
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:07,000
Every archive call has a fallback: Gaia through three
mirrors, asteroids through JPL and CADC, optical checks
through NEOSSat. Failures are reported, never hidden.

11
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Fourteen defect bits are rejected, but SOURCE and
FULLSAMPLE are kept — rejecting them erased real flux.
Masked pixels are dropped in both epochs, so a hole cannot
fake a dipole.

12
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:41,000
ZOGY convolves each image with the other's point-spread
function and divides by the combined noise — nothing is
fitted. The output is a flux difference and a significance
map that is unit-variance Gaussian wherever the sky did
not change. Two FFTs, under 0.6 seconds.

13
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:59,000
At five sigma the theory predicts 0.02 false pixels per
frame; the real data gave 0.86 per field. At six sigma
that halves, with no loss of recovery. The threshold is
calibrated, not inherited.

14
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Three shapes tell the story: a dipole means motion, a
positive peak means brightening, a negative peak means
fading. Geometry is the physics.

15
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:33,000
The turning point. Our first detector recovered 0.2 per
cent — three architectural defects: a global noise scale,
unconstrained dipole pairing, and a fixed bright-star
mask. Robust MAD scaling, physical pairing and exponential
masking took recovery to 69.3 per cent.

16
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:49,000
To measure false alarms we split one visit in two and
subtract: no real change can exist. Two hundred and twenty
realisations gave 8,854 raw peaks, cut to 1.66 false
candidates per field — zero in six star fields.

17
00:03:49,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Then we plant 12,240 sources and count: 96.7 per cent of
brightenings, 95.3 of fadings, 56 of movers — slow movers
hide inside the PSF. Overall: 69.3.

18
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Asteroids move arcminutes per hour and mimic flares, so we
identify them: JPL Horizons, the Small-Body Database, CADC
cross-checks — time-boxed at twenty-five seconds.

19
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Identity needs time: Gaia is epoch 2016, SPHEREx is 2026.
Propagating proper motion — Barnard's Star moves 94
arcseconds — turns a phantom candidate into KNOWN_MOVER.

20
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:46,000
A classifier on twenty-two features ranks candidates. The
headline 0.96 precision is inflated by synthetic dipoles;
honest monopole skill is 0.90 and 0.82. It ranks — it
never overrules a catalogue.

21
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Five weighted streams — machine learning, significance,
kinematics, asteroid clearance, optical confirmation —
combine into one explainable score.

22
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:07,000
At the ecliptic pole the same star is seen twenty thousand
eight hundred times. Spectral normalisation removes the
filter artefact: one of six stars variable, matching Gaia.

23
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:17,000
102 bands give a spectrum without follow-up: methane,
water and near-infrared slope separate brown dwarfs, icy
bodies and young stars — above thirty usable bands.

24
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:27,000
One FastAPI service, thin routers, a bounded job pool —
one running, eight waiting — rate limited and correlated.

25
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Reads and live work run in separate services: concurrency
eighty for snapshots, one for streaming, refreshed every
twelve hours.

26
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:47,000
The console is 7,611 lines of vanilla web code: no build
step, two-stage canvas, offline caching.

27
00:05:47,000 --> 00:06:05,000
The census: 109 detections across eighteen fields — 99
known, 10 candidates. Detection count is not discovery
count. Two fields return nothing: M42 is a null sky, Sgr
A-star has no shared band.

28
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:21,000
The ten sit in five fields — Epsilon Indi B, WISE 0855,
M45, TRAPPIST-1, 3C 273 — faint infrared excesses beyond
Gaia and WISE. Barnard's Star, our first candidate, was a
coordinate bug.

29
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Live: select Barnard's Star and run. A 36.2-sigma dipole,
carded as KNOWN_MOVER. Toggle co-add, difference,
significance. Play six months of snapshots. The Crab
Nebula for extended gas. The accuracy panel — every number
here comes from it.

30
00:06:45,000 --> 00:07:00,000
It works: 69.3 per cent recovery. It is honest: 1.66 false
candidates per field. And it is open: reproducible from
NASA's archive. Thank you.
